@* V1009_CLIENT_START_GUIDE *@
<section class="client-start-guide" aria-labelledby="client-start-title">
    <div class="client-start-guide-head">
        <div>
            <span class="client-start-kicker">YOUR FIRST WEEK</span>
            <h2 id="client-start-title">Learn the system while the system learns your pattern</h2>
        </div>
        <a asp-controller="Home"
           asp-action="Evidence"
           class="client-start-evidence-link">Why the evidence matters</a>
    </div>

    <p class="client-start-lead">
        12 Week Recomp is not a crash diet. It is a 12-week learning process
        designed to connect what you plan, what you actually do, what you eat,
        and how your body responds.
    </p>

    <div class="client-start-loop" aria-label="12 Week Recomp daily loop">
        <span>Plan</span>
        <b>&rarr;</b>
        <span>Do</span>
        <b>&rarr;</b>
        <span>Log</span>
        <b>&rarr;</b>
        <span>Measure</span>
        <b>&rarr;</b>
        <span>Learn</span>
        <b>&rarr;</b>
        <span>Adapt</span>
    </div>

    <div class="client-start-grid">
        <div>
            <strong>Weekly Plan = your intention</strong>
            <p>Set a week you can realistically complete. The plan gives Recomp context for what your body is being asked to do.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Daily Entry = what actually happened</strong>
            <p>Log the workout you really did and the food you really ate. An honest imperfect day is more useful than a perfect-looking estimate.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Morning scale = the next piece of evidence</strong>
            <p>When possible, measure under similar morning conditions. Yesterday's activity and nutrition are interpreted with the next morning's scale response.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Dashboard = interpretation, not judgment</strong>
            <p>Green, yellow, orange, red, and gray signals help explain the day. One number never tells the whole story.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Progress = the trend</strong>
            <p>Daily weight and BIA readings move with glycogen, water, sodium, inflammation, and food volume. Longer trends matter more than one morning.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>The goal = skills you keep</strong>
            <p>By the end of 12 weeks, you should understand how training, protein, carbohydrate, energy intake, recovery, and accurate tracking work together for you.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="client-start-priorities">
        <div>
            <strong>Hard training days</strong>
            <span>Protect protein and enough carbohydrate to restore useful glycogen. A modest calorie undershoot can still be a very good day.</span>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Easy cardio / recovery days</strong>
            <span>Energy control carries more weight. Lower carbohydrate is not automatically a problem when training demand is low.</span>
        </div>
        <div>
            <strong>Logging accuracy</strong>
            <span>The app can only reason from the evidence entered. Portions, cooking fats, sauces, restaurant estimates, snacks, and drinks are common places for calories to be missed.</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="client-start-note">
        <strong>What success looks like:</strong>
        consistency, useful training, adequate recovery, increasingly accurate
        tracking, and learning which habits you can repeat for life. Individual
        results vary; the objective is a sustainable process, not a one-day
        scale result.
    </div>
</section>